WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Errata checks every draft against your approved brand rules and cites the rule behind every flag.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:10.000
A creator writes a caption and sets its intent: goal, audience, call to action, channel and campaign.

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Six steps run. A flag survives only if it cites an approved rule or fact.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:20.000
CL-03: “assured returns” on a market-linked fund, quoting F-112 without its disclaimer.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
E-19: Oct 18 at Hall B belongs to another campaign. Caught without AI judgement.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
New since Round 2: six features, each one shown next.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
If the AI provider is busy, rule-based findings show at once and the AI check retries by itself.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.000
The image says Oct 18, the caption says Oct 28. Fact E-19 says Oct 28, so the image is flagged.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Low-contrast text gets the closest colour from your own palette that passes. Nothing changes until a person accepts it.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Claims go to Legal, who signs off with a reason. Every decision is audited.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:02.000
At publish-ready, Errata issues a receipt: a hash of the approved text and the facts it relies on.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.000
If one of those facts changes or expires, approval is revoked and the draft goes back for revision.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:13.000
Unclear rules get better examples. A backtest on recent real reviews projects the effect before anyone approves them.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Claude or Cursor can fetch live campaign facts and pre-check drafts, with a personal read-only key.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:27.000
Humans approve. Errata never publishes.
